#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:1
msgid "Compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:3
msgid ""
"cuTile Rust supports launch-time JIT compilation for normal kernel launches "
"and compile-only APIs for tooling and pre-compilation pipelines. In both "
"paths, the compiler resolves a kernel specialization, lowers it to Tile IR "
"bytecode, and invokes the CUDA Tile IR assembler to produce code for the "
"target GPU. A specialization is the concrete compiled variant for one entry "
"function, target architecture, and set of values that affect generated GPU "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:5
msgid ""
"![The cuTile Rust compilation pipeline from Rust to GPU execution](../"
"_static/images/compilation-pipeline.svg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:5
msgid "The cuTile Rust compilation pipeline from Rust to GPU execution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:7
msgid "Rust AST -> Tile IR bytecode -> cubin\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:11
msgid ""
"For launch-time JIT, the compiled kernel is cached in the host process. "
"Launching the same specialization again reuses the cached cubin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:13
msgid "Launch-Time JIT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:15
msgid ""
"For generated `#[cutile::entry]` launchers, compilation happens at first "
"launch, not when the Rust crate itself is built:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:17
msgid ""
"let op = add((&mut z).partition([16, 16]), &x, &y); // Builds a DeviceOp.\n"
"let _ = op.sync_on(&stream)?;                       // May compile, then "
"launch.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:22
msgid ""
"If the cache already contains a matching specialization, launch proceeds "
"without recompilation. Restarting the process clears the in-memory cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:24
msgid "What Gets Specialized"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:26
msgid ""
"Specialization works like Rust monomorphization: a generic function such as "
"`fn f<T, const N: usize>(...)` is compiled for each concrete `T` and `N`. "
"cuTile includes the entry function's type and const generic arguments in the "
"kernel cache key. Changing them produces a new GPU specialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:31
msgid "Common Recompilation Triggers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:33
msgid ""
"**Entry-function arguments.** Type and const generic arguments in the "
"`#[cutile::entry]` signature specialize the generated GPU code. Those values "
"are not always written next to `.generics(...)`; they can also be inferred "
"from host-side values passed to the launcher:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:35
msgid ""
"`z.partition([BM, BN])` can bind the tile shape for `&mut Tensor<T, { [BM, "
"BN] }>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:36
msgid ""
"`z.partition([BM, BN]).map([MAP_M, MAP_N], num_tile_blocks)` can bind the "
"output tile shape and the `MAP_SHAPE` for `MappedPartitionMut<T, { [BM, "
"BN] }, MAP_SHAPE>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:37
msgid ""
"Passing a tensor to a parameter with static or generic dimensions, such as "
"`&Tensor<T, { [K, -1] }>`, can bind those static dimensions from the tensor "
"shape. Use `-1` for dimensions that should vary without recompilation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:39
msgid ""
"For mapped partitions, `MAP_SHAPE` affects JIT because it is part of the "
"device-side type. `num_tile_blocks` is different: it controls launch-grid "
"inference for how many physical tile blocks traverse the mapped partition. "
"By itself, it does not create a new cache entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:41
msgid ""
"Runtime tensor data and runtime scalar values can change without "
"recompilation. Dynamic tensor dimensions (`-1`) are passed at runtime; the "
"exact dimension values are not entry-function generics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:43
msgid ""
"**Target architecture.** A cubin is generated for one target GPU "
"architecture. Running the same entry function on a different SM target "
"requires a different compiled artifact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:45
msgid "Explicit Compile-Time Inputs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:47
msgid ""
"The remaining user-controlled recompilation triggers are explicit. Use these "
"APIs only when the compiler should specialize on the value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:49
msgid ""
"`.const_grid((x, y, z))` embeds the launch grid as a compile-time value. "
"Changing it creates a separate specialization. Use `.grid(...)` when the "
"launch grid should remain runtime-only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:50
msgid ""
"`.compile_options(opts)` embeds tuning choices such as occupancy, "
"architecture-specific scheduling settings, and divisibility hints. Different "
"options produce separate cache entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:52
msgid ""
"Runtime inputs such as `.grid(...)`, scalar values, tensor contents, dynamic "
"dimensions (`-1`), and `MappedPartitionMut`'s `num_tile_blocks` do not "
"create cache entries by themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:57
msgid ""
"cuTile may also cache separate optimized variants for tensor specialization "
"hints derived from shape and stride metadata. This is a performance "
"tradeoff: the compiler can generate better code when it knows facts such as "
"power-of-two divisibility for shape dimensions and strides, or that a stride "
"is exactly `1`. Those facts can enable simpler indexing, stronger alignment "
"assumptions, and more efficient memory access. The hints affect cache reuse, "
"but they are not separate values passed to the kernel entry function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:60
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry()]\n"
"fn add<const S: [i32; 2]>(\n"
"    z: &mut Tensor<f32, S>,\n"
"    x: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
") {\n"
"    z.store(x.load_like(z));\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:70
msgid ""
"Changing `S` from `[16, 16]` to `[32, 32]` creates a new specialization. "
"Changing the runtime shape of `x` from `[1024, 1024]` to `[2048, 1024]` does "
"not change the entry-function generics because both dimensions are dynamic "
"in the kernel signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:72
msgid "Designing for Cache Reuse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:74
msgid ""
"Treat the entry function signature as the specialization boundary. Put "
"values in type or const generics only when changing those values should "
"produce different GPU code. Keep varying problem sizes as dynamic tensor "
"dimensions when the same compiled kernel should handle them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:76
msgid ""
"Tile shape dimensions are the main exception to the \"keep sizes dynamic\" "
"rule. Tile IR requires tile shapes to be compile-time constants, and cuTile "
"Rust exposes that through APIs such as `Tile<T, S>` and `tensor."
"partition(S)`. Dimensions such as `BM`, `BN`, and `BK` are often const "
"generics because changing them changes the tile type the compiler lowers to "
"Tile IR."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:78
msgid ""
"Tile shape dimensions and full tensor dimensions play different roles. For "
"mutable outputs, a signature such as `&mut Tensor<f32, { [BM, BN] }>` "
"usually receives a host-side `z.partition([BM, BN])`; the generic shape is "
"the output tile shape exposed to the kernel, not the full output tensor "
"shape. This is expected and does not create a specialization for every "
"problem size. For read-only tensors, generic shape dimensions describe the "
"tensor shape visible to the kernel. Use `-1` for input dimensions that are "
"expected to vary, and use generic/static input dimensions only when those "
"dimensions should be part of the specialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:80
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry()]\n"
"fn normalize<const BM: i32, const BN: i32>(\n"
"    z: &mut Tensor<f32, { [BM, BN] }>,\n"
"    x: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
") {\n"
"    let tile = x.load_like(z);\n"
"    let row_max = reduce_max(tile, 1i32);\n"
"    z.store(tile - row_max.reshape(shape![BM, 1]).broadcast(z.shape()));\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:92
msgid ""
"Changing `BM` or `BN` produces a new specialization because they appear in "
"the entry function signature. Changing the full shape of `x` can reuse the "
"same entry-function specialization because both dimensions are `-1` in the "
"signature; shape validation still runs at launch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:94
msgid ""
"Keep the set of tile shapes, element types, target architectures, const "
"grids, and compile options small in hot paths. Excessive specialization "
"increases first-launch latency and memory use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:96
msgid ""
"Tensor and scalar specialization hints are bucketed by power-of-two "
"divisibility. By default, auto-inferred hints are capped at `16`: dimensions "
"such as `16`, `32`, `64`, and `1024` all record the same `divisor = 16`, so "
"changing a dynamic dimension among values divisible by `16` does not create "
"a new cache entry through that dimension's divisibility hint. Changing from "
"a value divisible by `8` to one divisible by `16` can create a different "
"specialization hint and therefore a different cache entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:98
msgid ""
"`max_divisibility` controls the strongest divisibility assumption the "
"compiler emits from these hints. It is a ceiling, not a request to invent "
"stronger facts. For example, `max_divisibility = 4` makes a value known to "
"be divisible by `16` compile with a `div_by<4>` assumption. Changing "
"`max_divisibility` is itself a compile option change, so it creates a "
"separate cache entry. Values above `16` do not strengthen auto-inferred "
"hints today because inference is already capped at `16`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:100
msgid "Compile Options and Hints"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:102
msgid ""
"Entry-level optimization hints are written on the `#[cutile::entry]` "
"attribute:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:104
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry(\n"
"    optimization_hints = (\n"
"        sm_120 = (\n"
"            num_cta_in_cga = 2,\n"
"            occupancy = 2,\n"
"            max_divisibility = 16,\n"
"            num_worker_warps_per_cta = 4, // Bytecode 13.3+; valid values: "
"1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32.\n"
"        ),\n"
"        sm_90 = (\n"
"            num_cta_in_cga = 1,\n"
"        ),\n"
"    )\n"
")]\n"
"fn optimized_kernel<const S: [i32; 2]>(...) { ... }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:121
msgid ""
"The host can override these values with `CompileOptions`, which is useful "
"for autotuning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:123
msgid ""
"use cutile::tile_kernel::CompileOptions;\n"
"\n"
"let opts = CompileOptions::default()\n"
"    .occupancy(4)\n"
"    .num_cta_in_cga(2)\n"
"    .num_worker_warps_per_cta(4); // Bytecode 13.3+; valid values: 1, 2, 4, "
"8, 16, 32.\n"
"\n"
"let _ = optimized_kernel(args)\n"
"    .compile_options(opts)\n"
"    .sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:136
msgid ""
"`num_worker_warps_per_cta` requires bytecode version 13.3 or newer. It must "
"be a power of two in the inclusive range `[1, 32]` (`1`, `2`, `4`, `8`, "
"`16`, or `32`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:138
msgid "Different `CompileOptions` values produce separate JIT cache entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:140
msgid ""
"Per-operation hints are available on lower-level memory APIs when a specific "
"load or store needs a latency hint or a Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA) hint:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:142
msgid ""
"let tile = load_view_tko(\n"
"    &partition,\n"
"    idx,\n"
"    ordering::Weak,\n"
"    scope::TileBlock,\n"
"    Some(4),\n"
"    tma::Enabled,\n"
");\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:153
msgid ""
"Use hints to guide the compiler after measuring. The default compiler "
"choices are the right starting point for most kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:155
msgid "Kernel Cache Key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:157
msgid ""
"At launch time, cuTile looks for a cached compiled kernel using the active "
"device and the kernel specialization. The pattern below uses the internal "
"field names that define that lookup:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:159
msgid ""
"match TileFunctionKey {\n"
"    module_name,\n"
"    function_name,\n"
"    function_generics,\n"
"    stride_args,\n"
"    spec_args: [\n"
"        (tensor_param, SpecializationBits {\n"
"            shape_div,\n"
"            stride_div,\n"
"            stride_one,\n"
"            base_ptr_div,\n"
"            elements_disjoint,\n"
"        }),\n"
"        ...\n"
"    ],\n"
"    scalar_hints: [\n"
"        (integer_or_pointer_param, DivHint { divisor, max }),\n"
"        ...\n"
"    ],\n"
"    grid,\n"
"    compile_options,\n"
"    source_hash,\n"
"    device_id,\n"
"    gpu_name,\n"
"    compiler_version,\n"
"    tileiras_fingerprint,\n"
"} {\n"
"    cached => reuse_compiled_kernel(),\n"
"    missing => compile_and_insert_into_cache(),\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "Field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "What Changes It"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`module_name`, `function_name`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"The `#[cutile::module]` module and `#[cutile::entry]` function being "
"launched."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`function_generics`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"Type and const generic arguments for the entry function, including tile "
"shapes such as `BM`, `BN`, and `BK` when they are part of the signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`stride_args`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "The stride-one pattern recorded for tensor arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`spec_args`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"Tensor specialization hints. Today this includes per-dimension shape "
"divisibility, per-dimension stride divisibility, whether each stride is "
"exactly `1`, base-pointer divisibility, and a stride-derived non-overlap bit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`scalar_hints`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"`DivHint` values derived from integer scalar parameters and raw pointer "
"parameters. Integer hints are based on value divisibility; pointer hints are "
"based on pointer alignment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`grid`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"`Some((x, y, z))` only when `.const_grid(...)` is used. Runtime `.grid(...)` "
"leaves this as `None`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`compile_options`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"Per-launch compile options such as occupancy, architecture-specific "
"scheduling settings, and divisibility limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`source_hash`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"A hash of the module's source text, generated by `#[cutile::module]`. "
"Editing any kernel or device function in the module (and rebuilding) changes "
"it, so a stale compiled kernel is never reused across a source change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`device_id`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "The active CUDA device. The compiled artifact is loaded per device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`gpu_name`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"The target architecture (`sm_90`, `sm_120`, ...), inferred from the device "
"for normal launches. A cubin is generated for one architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`compiler_version`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "The `cutile-compiler` crate version that produced the entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid "`tileiras_fingerprint`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:56
msgid ""
"The output of `tileiras --version` for the resolved assembler binary "
"(falling back to its path, size, and mtime). Upgrading the toolkit, or "
"pointing `CUTILE_TILEIRAS_PATH` at a different binary, changes it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:207
msgid ""
"Values outside this pattern are ordinary launch or data inputs. Tensor "
"contents, floating-point scalar values, and runtime grid values passed with "
"`.grid(...)` do not create cache entries by themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:209
msgid "Persistent Disk Cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:211
msgid ""
"The kernel cache above lives in process memory and dies with the process. "
"cuTile can additionally persist compiled cubins to disk, so a fresh process "
"skips the `tileiras` backend for kernels any earlier process already "
"compiled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:213
msgid ""
"Disk persistence is **off by default, and no environment variable can turn "
"it on**. A program opts in explicitly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:215
msgid ""
"// ~/.cache/cutile/kernels ($XDG_CACHE_HOME respected), 2 GiB LRU target:\n"
"cutile::jit_cache::enable_default()?;\n"
"\n"
"// or a custom location and capacity:\n"
"use cutile::jit_cache::FileSystemJitStore;\n"
"let store = FileSystemJitStore::builder(\"/data/kernel-cache\")\n"
"    .capacity_bytes(512 << 20)\n"
"    .open()?;\n"
"cutile::jit_cache::enable(std::sync::Arc::new(store));\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:227
msgid ""
"`cutile::jit_cache::disable()` stops all disk reads and writes; kernels "
"already in the in-memory cache are unaffected. The `JitStore` trait behind "
"`enable` is a plain byte-oriented get/put interface, so a custom backend (an "
"object store, a network cache) can replace the filesystem implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:229
msgid ""
"A disk hit skips `tileiras`. The compiler frontend still runs on every in-"
"memory miss to validate the launch parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:232
msgid "The persistent key is a SHA-256 hash of the full `tileiras` input:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:234
msgid ""
"Bytecode version (`major.minor` and `tag`; see [Bytecode Version](#bytecode-"
"version))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:235
msgid "Serialized Tile IR bytecode, including inlined dependency modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:236
msgid "Target architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:237
msgid ""
"Optimization level and the stage-2 flags (`device_debug`, `lineinfo`, "
"`sanitize_memcheck`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:238
msgid "Resolved `tileiras` binary's fingerprint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:240
msgid ""
"Changes to these inputs invalidate old entries. Different assembler binaries "
"that report the same version are not distinguished."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:243
msgid ""
"Each entry has a checksummed header, validated on every hit. Incomplete, "
"corrupted, or mismatched entries are recompiled. The checksum does not "
"authenticate code: anyone with write access can create a valid entry "
"containing arbitrary device code. Restrict writes to custom and shared "
"stores to trusted users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:248
msgid ""
"Cache I/O errors are counted but do not fail a launch; compilation continues "
"without the disk cache. To inspect cache use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:251
msgid ""
"`cutile::jit_cache::stats()` — hits, misses, entries written, bytes written, "
"soft I/O errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:252
msgid ""
"`cutile::jit_cache::jit_backend_compile_count()` / `jit_disk_hit_count()` — "
"with `jit_compile_count()`, these satisfy `compiles == backend + disk_hits` "
"absent failures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:253
msgid ""
"`CUTILE_JIT_TIMING=1` — the per-kernel timing line includes "
"`stage2_source=disk|tileiras`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:255
msgid ""
"The eviction policy is LRU by file mtime with a high/low watermark pair "
"(defaults: collect above capacity, delete oldest entries down to 80%). The "
"configured capacity is a soft target, not a hard cap: eviction is "
"probabilistically triggered and uses a non-blocking lock, so transient "
"overshoot is possible. Multiple processes can share one cache directory; "
"writes are atomic and eviction is coordinated through a lock file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:257
msgid ""
"Concurrent processes may each compile the same missing entry. Warm the "
"shared cache in one process before starting parallel workers to avoid "
"duplicate work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/jit-compilation.md:260
msgid ""
"See `cutile-examples/examples/jit_disk_cache.rs`; run it twice to watch the "
"second process hit the disk. For implementing a custom backend (an object "
"store, a database), see `cutile-examples/examples/jit_custom_store.rs`."
msgstr ""

